# Project Cognition Report — commander.js

**Snapshot analyzed:** commit `bf35c5f` (detached HEAD, working tree clean) — v14.0.3 plus two Dependabot dev-dependency bumps (`typescript-eslint` 8.48.0→8.54.0, `eslint-plugin-jest` 29.2.1→29.12.1). No uncommitted changes.

---

## (1) Project Identity

- **What it is:** `commander` — "the complete solution for node.js command-line interfaces." A zero-dependency Node.js library for building CLIs: option/argument declaration, parsing, subcommand dispatch (in-process and standalone-executable), automated help, error handling, and TypeScript typings.
- **Origin/ownership:** Created by TJ Holowaychuk; MIT-licensed; maintained at `github.com/tj/commander.js`. One of the most widely installed npm packages.
- **Snapshot version:** `14.0.3` (released 2026-01-31), on the current 14.x line. Prior majors: 13.x (maintenance until 2026-05-18), <13 end-of-life.
- **Runtime contract:** CommonJS (`type: "commonjs"`, `main: index.js`) with an ESM wrapper (`esm.mjs`), dual `exports` map, and hand-maintained TypeScript declarations (`typings/index.d.ts`, `typings/esm.d.mts`). Requires **Node.js ≥ 20**.
- **Philosophy (explicit in CONTRIBUTING.md):** **zero production dependencies is a project policy**, not an accident — dependency-adding PRs are "much less likely to be accepted."
- **Package shape:** ~87 KB of runtime code in `lib/` (6 files), 43 KB README (+ Chinese translation), 109 Jest test files (~11k lines) plus ESM/TS import tests, ~50 examples, 6 docs pages (+ zh-CN translations), CI on Node 20/22/24 × Linux/Windows/macOS.
- **Cadence:** ~6-month majors, aligned to Node LTS end-of-life; semver with migration tips in the changelog; new release-policy document (added in 14.0.3) formalizes: current line gets features/security fixes, older lines get security fixes for 12 months.

---

## (2) Architecture Map

```
index.js  ── CJS entry: exports.program (singleton Command), factories
  │           (createCommand/createOption/createArgument), classes, errors
esm.mjs   ── ESM wrapper: re-exports named bindings from index.js
lib/
  command.js        (2777 lines)  Command class + helpers (useColor, inspector-port bump)
  option.js         (380 lines)   Option class, DualOptions, splitOptionFlags/camelcase
  argument.js       (150 lines)   Argument class, humanReadableArgName
  help.js           (747 lines)   Help class: layout, wrapping, styling (41 methods)
  error.js          ( 39 lines)   CommanderError, InvalidArgumentError
  suggestSimilar.js (101 lines)   Damerau–Levenshtein "did you mean" suggestions
typings/            index.d.ts (1113 lines) + esm.d.mts + index.test-d.ts (tsd)
tests/              109 *.test.js + esm-imports-test.mjs + ts-imports.test.ts
docs/               help-in-depth, options-in-depth, parsing-and-hooks,
                    release-policy, terminology, deprecated (+ zh-CN mirror)
examples/           ~50 runnable snippets (pizza, deploy, pm, custom-help, ...)
.github/workflows/  tests.yml (matrix build), codeql-analysis.yml
```

**Execution architecture** — the parse pipeline (the heart of the system):

```
parse() / parseAsync()
  → _prepareForParse()        save/restore state (multi-parse support since v13)
  → _prepareUserArgs()        argv-convention detection: node | electron | user | eval
  → _parseCommand(operands, unknown)
      → parseOptions()        ← tokenizer/state machine (options vs operands vs unknown)
      → _parseOptionsEnv()    env vars fill values only if source is default/config/env
      → _parseOptionsImplied() applies .implies() values via DualOptions
      → subcommand dispatch: _findCommand → _dispatchSubcommand
          ├─ in-process: subCommand._parseCommand (recursive)
          └─ executable:  _executeSubCommand → child_process.spawn, signal forwarding
      → _checkNumberOfArguments() → _processArguments() (parseArg per declared argument)
      → hooks preAction → action handler → legacy parent emit → hooks postAction
```

**Key structural facts:**

- **`Command extends EventEmitter`** — the internal dispatch mechanism is stringly-typed events: options register `option:<name>` and `optionEnv:<name>` listeners at declaration time; the tokenizer emits them; legacy `command:<name>` events are emitted on the parent. This is the library's central coupling point.
- **Two subcommand styles:** action-handler commands (chained API returns the child) and standalone-executable commands (second-arg description; dispatch spawns `name-subname` files with `.js/.ts/.tsx/.mjs/.cjs` resolution and local-file-first search).
- **Value model:** every option value is tracked alongside its source (`_optionValues` + `_optionValueSources`; sources: `default < config < env < cli` priority, plus `implied`), enabling env/implies precedence rules without re-parsing.
- **Extension model:** factory methods (`createCommand/createOption/createArgument/createHelp`) so subclasses can swap in custom classes; `configureHelp`/`configureOutput`/`helpOption`/`addHelpText` for behavior overrides; `exitOverride` for embedding.
- **Layering:** runtime JS is written as modern CJS with rich JSDoc (checked by `tsc --checkJs`); types live separately in `typings/index.d.ts` and are validated by `tsd` + `tsc` (dual-source-of-truth, deliberately kept in sync).

---

## (3) Critical Components

1. **`lib/command.js` — the Command monolith (highest criticality).** Registration (`command()`, `addCommand`, `_registerOption/_registerCommand` with conflict detection), the parse pipeline (`parseOptions`, `_parseCommand`, `_processArguments`, `_checkNumberOfArguments`), dispatch (`_dispatchSubcommand`, `_dispatchHelpCommand`, default command, `*` legacy), executable spawning (`_executeSubCommand`, `_checkForMissingExecutable`), value plumbing (`setOptionValueWithSource`, `opts()`, `optsWithGlobals()`), error/exit (`error()`, `_exit()`, `exitOverride`), and help/version/output (`helpInformation`, `outputHelp`, `_outputHelpIfRequested`, `version`, `nameFromFilename`). ~99 methods in one class.

2. **`parseOptions()` tokenizer — the parsing core.** Handles `--` literal, short-flag groups (`-abc`), combined short-flag values (`-p80`), `--long=value`, optional/required/variadic value consumption, negative-number auto-detection (`-1`, `-1.5e-3` allowed unless a digit short-flag exists in the command hierarchy), unknown-option sinking, and `enablePositionalOptions`/`passThroughOptions` short-circuits. Regression-prone; heavily tested (`command.parseOptions.test.js`).

3. **Option/Argument declaration model.** `Option` parses flag strings via `splitOptionFlags` (single short, one or two longs, negated `--no-` detection, strict throw on malformed flags since v13); `Argument` parses `<required>`/`[optional]`/`variadic...` shapes. Both support `default`, `choices`, `argParser`, variadic `_collectValue`, and (Option-only) `preset`, `conflicts`, `implies`, `env`, `makeOptionMandatory`, `helpGroup`.

4. **`Help` subsystem (`lib/help.js`).** 41 methods spanning visibility filtering (with implicit help option/command injection), term formatting, item-list layout (`formatItemList`/`groupItems` from the 14.0.0 refactor), wrapping (`boxWrap`, `formatItem`, `displayWidth`, `minWidthToWrap`), group headings, and the style-* color routines; consumed via `Command.helpInformation()/_getOutputContext()` with color stripping for non-TTY output.

5. **Executable subcommand machinery.** `_executeSubCommand` resolves files (realpath of script, legacy script-name prefix search), decides `node` launch for source extensions, increments `--inspect*` ports to avoid collisions, forwards SIGUSR1/2, SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP, and calls `process.exit(code)` on close unless `exitOverride` is set. Windows goes through `process.execPath` with an explicit missing-executable check.

6. **Error/exit model.** `CommanderError(exitCode, code, message)` with stable codes (`commander.missingArgument`, `commander.invalidArgument`, `commander.unknownOption`, `commander.helpDisplayed`, …); `InvalidArgumentError`; `_callParseArg` wraps user parseArg throws; `exitOverride` is the sanctioned embedding hook. `useColor()` implements NO_COLOR/FORCE_COLOR/CLICOLOR conventions manually.

7. **Typings (`typings/index.d.ts`).** 1113 lines, hand-written; `LiteralUnion` trick for help text positions; overloaded `Command` API; validated by `index.test-d.ts` (tsd) and by `tsc` against the JS (`tsconfig.js.json`). Type drift is a real maintenance surface — every runtime feature lands here too.

8. **`suggestSimilar.js`.** Optimal-string-alignment (Damerau–Levenshtein, max distance 3, min similarity 0.4) powering "Did you mean" for unknown options/commands.

